          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I ate shellfish ten minutes ago and now my lips are swollen and my throat feels tight. Should I use my adrenaline pen?",
          "answer": "**This is an emergency. Call emergency services now.** Swollen lips and a tight throat within minutes of eating shellfish can be signs of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction that can close the airway. Get immediate emergency care.\n\n**Should you use the adrenaline pen?** Yes, if you have one. The guidance says that where an adrenaline auto-injector is available and anaphylaxis is suspected, it should be used immediately into the outer thigh, before or while emergency services are called. Follow the device's own instructions. Delay in giving adrenaline is the factor most consistently linked to fatal outcomes. Antihistamines and inhalers do not treat the airway swelling of anaphylaxis, so do not use them in place of adrenaline (Source: red-flag-anaphylaxis).\n\n**Call emergency services right away, whatever you decide about the pen.** Do not wait to see whether the symptoms improve. If someone is with you, ask them to make the call while you use the pen. Tell the responders when the symptoms started and what you ate.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
